+      "content": "# Embedded Skill audit — 11.3.6 baseline\n\nBaseline: `e13bd4788d5638c1eb34c169870f19dfd5285b6b`. Read all 25 assigned Skill entries and all 16 Skill-local reference files. Exact paths, SHA-256 values, byte sizes and per-Skill retain/change reasons are in `portfolio-audit.json`. This is a source audit, not hardware qualification.\n\n## Confirmed changes\n\n### E1 — CTest can return success with no passing test\n\nThe current `adk-cmake-cross-build` manual block was executed verbatim using existing CMake/CTest 3.31.6 binaries and a native `project(... NONE)` fixture. A single test running `cmake -E false` with `SKIP_RETURN_CODE 1` makes the command return **0**, while CTest prints `100% tests passed` and lists its sole test as `Skipped`. The actual JUnit result records one selected test, one skipped test, status `notrun`, and no passing test.\n\nThe CMake and HIL/SIL entries already state that skip is not pass, but their executable examples provide no actual result artifact or qualification step. CMake needs same-invocation, newly created JUnit evidence plus a result check; HIL/SIL needs a fresh report and an explicit distinction between command exit and test qualification. Preserve normal failure codes, missing-backend `not-run`, configuration/selector identity, and all required-case obligations. A mixed pass/skip run is partial, not blanket acceptance.\n\nEvidence: `ctest-counterexamples/receipt.json`, especially `skipped-valid-command/{stdout.log,stderr.log,result.xml}`.\n\n### E2 — Timeout diagnostics lose captured output and leave descendants running\n\nThe original `tests/test_skill_command_examples.py:run_example` was exercised with its real **15 second** timeout. It captured `configure complete` on stdout and `build waiting` on stderr, but `TimeoutExpired`'s displayed string omitted both. Its `sleep 60` descendant was still alive after the helper timed out. The isolated probe became a subreaper and explicitly killed/reaped that child; it left no active process behind.\n\nThe helper should isolate its owned process group, preserve output and execution identity on timeout, terminate that group, and bound cleanup separately. The entire example must still have one 15 second work budget with no retries. Add real subprocess tests for normal output/status, both captured streams on timeout, child cleanup and unrelated-process survival. This improves diagnosis; it does **not** establish the root cause of the earlier release runner timeout.\n\nEvidence: `timeout-counterexample/receipt.json` (elapsed 15.0218 seconds).\n\n## Negative results and retained behavior\n\n- The broader hypothesis that all disabled tests also false-pass was **disproved for CMake 3.31.6 with `--no-tests=error`**: the fixture returned 8. Empty selection also returned 8. Keep these as protected negatives, not new defect claims.\n- The initial skip fixture referred to an absent `/usr/local/bin/python`, yielding an ordinary missing-executable failure. It is preserved as an invalid harness experiment. The corrected fixture uses the already verified CMake executable and a declared skip return code; that is the confirmed counterexample.\n- `rtk`, PATH CMake/CTest and Ninja are unavailable. Existing CMake/CTest binaries were found under `/workspace/scratch/771b38332ec2/domain-audit-tools/cmake-wheel/cmake/data/bin`, version 3.31.6, and their SHA-256 values are in the receipt. No tool installation was performed.\n- `knowledge/L2-domain/lessons.md` is absent; no historical lesson was invented.\n- The remaining 23 owned Skills are retained with individual evidence-based reasoning. Their prior runtime distinctions, artifact identity, memory ownership, deadline, API/ABI, stateful oracle and statistical limits were inspected rather than rewritten for appearance.\n\nNo board/HIL experiment, ARM build, physical measurement, external inference API or model CLI was used. The native fixture demonstrates CTest semantics only.\n"
