+      "content": "# General/optional Skill portfolio audit\n\nAudit role: read-only auditor. Baseline: `e13bd4788d5638c1eb34c169870f19dfd5285b6b` (11.3.6). Read all 40 owned Skill entries, 33 direct references and four additional bundled references (37 resources total); the per-path baseline SHA256, inspection scope and retain/change rationale are in `portfolio-audit.json`. No repository edit or external model/runtime invocation occurred during the audit phase. After independent design preapproval, the same agent switched to implementer for the frozen T3 write set; implementation receipts are separate. `rtk` and `knowledge/L2-domain/lessons.md` are unavailable; raw tools were used and no historical lesson was invented.\n\n## G1 — Parallel worker prompt depends on an absent exported source aid\n\n`skills/adk-parallel-agent-governance/SKILL.md` Execute/Collect/Integrate step 2 unconditionally asks for `templates/planning/worker-contract.md`. Its bundled `references/parallel-worktree-task-package.md` repeats that instruction. Actual exports through `export_assets` to both `claude-code` and `opencode` succeeded, included the Skill and local task-package reference, but contained no source aid at either consumer root or Skill root. The source-only path cannot be the default consumer workflow.\n\nMinimal fix: make the already bundled reference the default when a worker is actually being dispatched; use the repository helper only when an ADK source root and the helper file have been verified. Preserve v2 permission, scope, report schema, raw retention, redaction, parent merge policy and integration requirements. There is no need for a new copied template or a second task schema.\n\nAcceptance: export both actual consumer packages; resolve the default package from the exported Skill, validate all required field categories, and show that the source aid is absent yet optional. No package default may require access to the source repository. Source-contract proof only; native model behavior remains unmeasured.\n\n## G2 — Secondary review examples retain the index-mutating commands fixed in the main template\n\n`skills/adk-code-review-loop/references/review-feedback-fixtures.md` still publishes `git status --short`, `git write-tree` and unqualified `git diff` in Snapshot Commands. In an isolated real Git repository with unchanged tracked content and a changed mtime, the first published command exits 0 and changes the index SHA256 from `d1749baf60202cf6cc9080b87d984bb0ff183eb57e445441d3b94600c30c0ff6` to `3807218d87e6deb4d579abc45950198969d2664710b3242c6a0adf008ee1b145`. The main `review-evidence-template.md` already uses `--no-optional-locks` and `diff.autoRefreshIndex=false`; the alternative examples bypass that fix.\n\nMinimal fix: remove the duplicate Snapshot Commands block and direct readers to the existing canonical snapshot reference, including its staged/working-tree/whole-branch target choice. Keep mechanical-vs-semantic and re-review semantics. This removes stale instructions and reduces reference bytes.\n\nAcceptance: inspect the actual exported feedback reference; resolve its canonical command source and execute its snapshot commands on the real mtime-only fixture. The index and tracked content must remain byte-identical. Retain existing staged/unstaged/branch/overlay assertions; do not replace them with slogan matching.\n\n## G3 — Growth-review metadata and README contradict the bounded default\n\n`skills/adk-engineering-growth-review/SKILL.md` frontmatter says local reads are broadly allowed and not limited to one project. Its Workflow and `growth-evidence-boundaries.md` instead default to index-first, current/user-provided scope and require an explicit cross-project/full-history request before expansion. `README.md` repeats the broad-read default. These surfaces can produce conflicting task selection and execution instructions.\n\nMinimal fix: align the frontmatter and README to the existing bounded body. Preserve explicit cross-project/full-history use when authorized; do not restrict legitimate requested scope or impose a fixed 24–48 h window.\n\nAcceptance: source/metadata contract across frontmatter, body and README has one default and explicitly preserves the expanded authorized path. This is a static contradiction fix, not measured proof of fewer model/tool calls.\n\n## G4 — Data-fetch parent loses the child result-state vocabulary\n\n`optional-skills/adk-data-fetch/SKILL.md` exposes only pass/needs-fix and says data acquisition must succeed. Both child Skills now distinguish partial, and IMAP also uses not-verified for uncertain read-only semantics. The parent has no rule preserving successful items plus missing/error/unverified scope, creating a gap when a child returns a partial result.\n\nMinimal fix: propagate child status without upgrading partial/unverified to pass, retain successful items and item-level errors/omissions, distinguish complete requested scope from an empty or incomplete result, and keep the installation/authorization boundary unchanged. No new fetching implementation or real mailbox/network action is needed.\n\nAcceptance: check a parent/child status matrix for complete, partial, not-verified and hard failure; parent must not discard good items or collapse partial to complete. Any task demonstration without a real connected source is a source-level walkthrough, not native fetch qualification.\n\n## G5 — Preserve unexplained archive hash differences before repair\n\nThe archive recovery workflow listed `hash mismatch` and then unconditionally included `update hash` among minimal repairs. This is a static design counterexample: when the content change is unexplained, recomputing its checksum would erase the observable discrepancy without establishing that the content is authorized. No actual customer archive incident was reproduced.\n\nRoot's approved design adds the smallest clarification to the existing step: preserve the old hash, measured hash and current content, compare a trusted original and approved changes, recompute only for a confirmed authorized edit, and retain `needs-fix` for an unknown difference. The regression checks the published source contract; it does not claim an archive engine, consumer execution or production integrity certification.\n\n## Retain decisions\n\nThe other 35 entries have specific retain rationales in the inventory. Their current methods already cover the identified neighboring issues (self-contained context handoff, stable question IDs, complete release history, independent expected values, explicit approval continuity, MIME/UID/SSRF, task-timeout versus infrastructure failure). The four additional bundled resources are self-contained templates/briefs and are retained. No cosmetic edits, duplicate runtime/router, budget-cap increase or new external provider was introduced.\n\nRaw deterministic evidence: `baseline-repros.json`. Reviewer conclusions and promotion remain the parent task's responsibility.\n"
